ve  about 17 000 people on the waiting list  so we had to look into the private  rented sector we've all been concerned  that the desperate situation unfolding  in afghanistan and we must do all we can  to thank them for the support and the  commitment they have shown to the uk but  to do this we need landlords to offer us  properties  so we can so for our scheme we came up  with a support package for landlords so  that they would feel that the risk they  take  in  offering a tenancy to a newly arrived  resettled refugee is manageable if the  landlord  can offer us a rental property which is  of good quality at a rent which is at a  local housing allowance level which is  set by government and it's um the amount  that will be paid for rent from benefits  from universal credit  then their work as a landlord can be  minimized because we do the work so we  find the tenant we furnish the property  we clean it all up and we get it ready  it's a package which enables the  landlord to receive less money each  month  but it's an easy life the sudden full of  kabul and emergency evacuations meant  many more people were coming to bristol  than previously expected the council was  asked to help him wrap around care  bespoke support to help familiarize  refugees with their local area  everything was changing very quickly and  i went to my team to say can somebody  help me to support roundabout i think it  was 250 afghan um people who'd arrived  as family units into the two bristol  hotels we knew that the most important  thing was relationship so people needed  to feel that there was somebody in the  uk who's looking out for them like it's  okay we've got you we're going to walk  alongside you you know we're going to  support you  in in your new life  so myself and um a team of nurses  and interpreters  we sat down in the hotels and we met  each of the families and we said how are  you do you have any medical issues  do you have any needs are you pregnant  how old are the children so that we  could look at schools we spoke to the  hotel management around appropriate food  and there was also  somebody from the home office was  allocated and the home office also  organized for the local dwp the job  centre  to also come in and help with doing all  the benefit claims on a monday we would  always offer a drop in and we would  answer every question that people wanted  so for  all the refugees who are referred to us  on a resettlement scheme so they're all  referred to us through the government  then they arrive with  immigration status as the right to  health the right to education the right  to work and the right to receive welfare  benefits  so across all of the schemes  then we will support people to register  with a gp we will support people to  claim universal credit and child benefit  if relevant we will support people to  register the children into schools we  will support the adults to register into  english classes  we will show them where the local shops  are  and we show people how to use transport  we also offer people  bus passes for two months so that  they're able to get around the city  [Music]  and with a right to work some local  employers have been visiting the hotels  and job fairs to advertise opportunities  to the refugees  many are becoming restless at the hotel  and are keen to find employment  some have come from high power jobs  while others didn't have a formal  education but have practical skills  some have excellent english others don't  have any  though language classes are being  offered as part of resettlement she said  just put me in like a kitchen i have  experience in the kitchen my name is  steve cream i'm the registered care home  manager at saffron gardens working for  brunellcare we've come to a hotel today  we're working with a charity to  help get afghan refugees who are looking  to settle in bristol um into work  there's no big need for experience  we have roles in  care  i manage a care for people living with  dementia we also have support roles such  as housekeeping and kitchen assistant  laundry assistant more task-based less  english needed we can show and they can  learn by doing  there needs to be a reasonable level of  english so they can communicate with the  residents it is  after all working in their home  they've been through a lot we need to  help them establish some sense of  normality give them some worth um give  them a feeling that they're being  accepted and they're moving forward with  their lives it's something that we'd all  want in the same situation so trying to  make things as normal as possible for  people  afzal told us he was proud but also  relieved when he landed a job at the  port  they told me when you want to start job  i told them  from today  but it is good for me because every  morning i wake up about 10 o'clock or 11  o'clock and it was my habit  lazy every day by day a day be blizzy  in hotel  and all the day i was  not very good not feel very good  but jobs also let them earn an income  work with new people and learn new  skills  they import and export the new cars by  merchant ship  i am a driver i drive the car from ship  to the to the car park and from car park  to the ship and there is a lot of  difference new car and right now i  understand about all the vehicles to you  see in bristol  i can't drive  all of them if i apply for the british  license because i have afghani  international license that's very easy  for me  shibano is running a sewing class for  some of the afghan women at a community  center  she says that many of these women are  uneducated  they bring their children along to these  classes  a prayer mat in the corner allows them  to observe their religious practices  last class was for  just measurement to take measurement of  the body and now i'm trying to show them  how to put the measurement on the paper  or fabric and cut it and they will learn  it they are trying to learn it and start  their own business or something  at home yeah and also if they don't  start business they can do for  themselves they can make clothes because  it's very expensive in uk  this isn't new to shivano back in  afghanistan she also dedicated her time  to training women and girls to better  their lives i started from my own house  like  i just made a one room like on the  terrace we had a  very big room and i put machines  everything and i called just uh near to  my house the people who were poor people  and  and the girls they come  the stuff related to sewing i would buy  for them and then they come to learn it  and if some people they have much  invariable machining they couldn't use  it and i take it and fix it by the  mechanic and bring it to one lady girl  or lady to give it to them it was very  important normally in afghanistan the  woman can't do outside work like start a  shop if you go to afghanistan you will  never see a lady in the shop to selling  something i was trying to teach them my  skill and they could do their business  from home  it is not a beginner class  it is  made to measure they know how to stitch  in a little bit cutting but they are  they are saying we don't know  professionally to take the measurement  of someone  it's moving day yousef shibano their  daughter and nephew are moving into a  new three-bedroom flat a couple of miles  away from the hotel in bristol  back in afghanistan they also had an  apartment but in a block with extended  family  in afghanistan all homes  is too like buildings yeah like flats  but we we get the whole building and we  have four floors to live on first floor  i have my cousin in there the second  floor was my father's uncle  and the third floor was my  uncle's the old uncles they lived  together and the top of floor is ours  we have pigeons on our  roof yeah we keep them and they live  with us  we fly them  they go around over our home like this  carrying the stuff upstairs  their new flat in bristol is split over  two floors 